Q 1. What is a functional interface? What is the relationship between a functional interface and a Lambda expression?

Demonstrate it using your own functional interface.





1. Functional Interface
A functional interface is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method. It is also called a Single Abstract Method (SAM) interface.

A functional interface can contain multiple default and static methods because they already have implementations.

Example:
@FunctionalInterface
interface Calculator {
    int calculate(int a, int b);
}


2. @FunctionalInterface
@FunctionalInterface is an annotation that tells the compiler that an interface is intended to be a functional interface.

The compiler verifies that the interface contains exactly one abstract method.


3. Lambda Expression
A Lambda expression is a concise way to provide the implementation of the single abstract method of a functional interface.

Syntax:
(parameters) -> expression

Example:
(a, b) -> a + b


4. Relationship Between Functional Interface and Lambda
The functional interface defines the contract, while the Lambda expression provides the implementation of that contract.

Example:
Calculator calculator = (a, b) -> a + b;

Calculator
    → Functional Interface / Target Type

calculate()
    → Single Abstract Method

(a, b) -> a + b
    → Lambda implementation


5. Single Abstract Method (SAM)
SAM means Single Abstract Method.

A functional interface must contain exactly one abstract method.


6. Method Reference
A method reference is a shorthand alternative to a Lambda expression when an existing method can provide the required implementation.

Example:
Calculator calculator = MathUtils::add;

Equivalent Lambda:
Calculator calculator = (a, b) -> MathUtils.add(a, b);


7. Common Java Functional Interfaces
Predicate<T>
    → Takes one argument and returns boolean.

Function<T, R>
    → Takes one argument and returns a result.

Consumer<T>
    → Takes one argument and returns nothing.

Supplier<T>
    → Takes no argument and returns a result.

UnaryOperator<T>
    → Takes one argument and returns the same type.

BinaryOperator<T>
    → Takes two arguments and returns the same type.



