    A functional interface is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method. It is also known as a Single Abstract Method, or SAM, interface.

We can use the @FunctionalInterface annotation to explicitly indicate that an interface is intended to be functional, and the compiler will verify that it contains only one abstract method.

The relationship between a functional interface and a Lambda expression is that the functional interface provides the target type and defines the contract, while the Lambda expression provides the implementation of its single abstract method.

For example:

@FunctionalInterface
interface Calculator {
    int calculate(int a, int b);
}

Now I can implement it using a Lambda:

Calculator calculator = (a, b) -> a + b;

Here, Calculator is the functional interface, calculate() is the single abstract method, and (a, b) -> a + b is the Lambda expression that provides its implementation.

So, in simple terms:

Functional Interface → Defines the contract
Lambda Expression   → Provides the implementation

This allows us to represent behavior concisely without creating a separate implementation class.

