Q 1. Design a generic Repository<T> class that can store and retrieve objects of different types. Explain why Generics are better than using Object.


1. Generics
Generics are a Java language feature that allows classes, interfaces, and methods to operate on parameterized types while providing compile-time type safety.

Example:
Repository<Employee>

Here, Employee is the type parameter supplied to Repository<T>.


2. Type Parameter
A type parameter is a placeholder for a type that is specified when a generic class, interface, or method is used.

Example:
class Repository<T> {
    private T data;
}

Here, T is the type parameter.


3. Generic Class
A generic class is a class that is declared with one or more type parameters.

Example:
class Repository<T> {
    private T data;

    public void save(T data) {
        this.data = data;
    }

    public T find() {
        return data;
    }
}


4. Parameterized Type
A parameterized type is a generic type used with a concrete type argument.

Examples:
Repository<Employee>
Repository<Product>
Repository<String>


5. Type Safety
Type safety means that the compiler can detect invalid type assignments at compile time rather than allowing them to fail later at runtime.

Example:

Repository<Employee> employeeRepository = new Repository<>();

employeeRepository.save(new Employee());

The repository accepts Employee objects only.


6. Compile-Time Type Checking
Generics allow Java to verify type compatibility during compilation.

Example:

Repository<Employee> repository = new Repository<>();

repository.save(new Product());   // Compilation error

This prevents incompatible objects from being inserted.


7. Type Erasure
Java implements generics using type erasure.

Generic type information is primarily used by the compiler for type checking and is erased from most generic type operations at runtime.

Example:

Repository<Employee>
Repository<Product>

Both use the same generic class implementation at runtime after type erasure.


8. Object
Object is the root class of the Java class hierarchy.

A variable of type Object can reference objects of any reference type.

Example:

Object value = new Employee();
Object value2 = new Product();


9. Generics vs Object

Using Object:
    → Can store different types
    → Loses specific type information at the API level
    → Requires explicit casting when retrieving values
    → Invalid types may be discovered at runtime

Using Generics:
    → Can store different types through parameterization
    → Preserves type information at compile time
    → Usually eliminates explicit casting
    → Invalid types are detected at compile time


10. Casting
Casting is explicitly converting a reference from one type to another compatible type.

Example:

Object value = new Employee();

Employee employee = (Employee) value;

With generics:

Repository<Employee> repository = new Repository<>();

Employee employee = repository.find();

No explicit cast is required.


11. Generic Repository
A generic Repository<T> is a reusable class that provides storage and retrieval operations for objects of a specified type.

Example:

Repository<Employee>
    → Stores and retrieves Employee objects

Repository<Product>
    → Stores and retrieves Product objects


12. Encapsulation in a Repository
A repository should encapsulate its internal storage and expose controlled operations such as save(), find(), and findAll() instead of allowing direct manipulation of its internal data structure.

👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉Interview Response




I would design Repository<T> as a generic class so that the same repository implementation can work with different object types while maintaining compile-time type safety.

For example:

class Repository<T> {

    private final List<T> items = new ArrayList<>();

    public void save(T item) {
        items.add(item);
    }

    public T find(int index) {
        return items.get(index);
    }

    public List<T> findAll() {
        return new ArrayList<>(items);
    }
}

Now I can use the same Repository class for different types:

Repository<Employee> employeeRepository = new Repository<>();
employeeRepository.save(new Employee());

Repository<Product> productRepository = new Repository<>();
productRepository.save(new Product());

When I retrieve an object, the compiler already knows its type:

Employee employee = employeeRepository.find(0);
Product product = productRepository.find(0);

The main advantage over using Object is type safety.

If I used Object, I would have to write something like:

List<Object> items = new ArrayList<>();

items.add(new Employee());

Employee employee = (Employee) items.get(0);

This requires explicit casting and can result in a ClassCastException at runtime if the wrong type is stored or retrieved.

With generics:

Repository<Employee> employeeRepository = new Repository<>();

employeeRepository.save(new Product());  // Compilation error

The compiler catches the problem before the application runs.

So, in an interview, I would summarize it as:

Generics → Compile-time type safety, reusable code, fewer casts, better readability.

Object → Can store anything, but loses type-specific information and requires runtime casting.

Therefore, for a Repository design, I would prefer Repository<T> because it provides a reusable and type-safe API while allowing the same implementation to work with Employee, Product, Customer, or any other object type.





