The difference between ? extends T and ? super T is primarily about whether the generic collection is producing values or consuming values.

? extends T is an upper-bounded wildcard. It means the collection contains some unknown type that is T or a subclass of T.

I use ? extends T when I need to READ from a collection.

For example:

static double sum(List<? extends Number> numbers) {
    double total = 0;

    for (Number number : numbers) {
        total += number.doubleValue();
    }

    return total;
}

This method can accept:

List<Integer>
List<Double>
List<Float>

because all of them extend Number.

The collection is producing Number values for us, so we use ? extends Number.


On the other hand, ? super T is a lower-bounded wildcard. It means the collection contains some unknown type that is T or a superclass of T.

I use ? super T when I need to ADD objects into a collection.

For example:

static void addIntegers(List<? super Integer> numbers) {
    numbers.add(10);
    numbers.add(20);
}

This method can accept:

List<Integer>
List<Number>
List<Object>

because all of these can safely store an Integer.

The collection is consuming Integer values, so we use ? super Integer.


The important interview rule is PECS:

Producer Extends
Consumer Super

So:

READ from collection → ? extends T
ADD to collection  → ? super T


The reason ? extends T cannot safely accept additions is that the actual subtype is unknown.

For example:

List<? extends Number> numbers;

The actual list could be List<Integer> or List<Double>. If Java allowed us to add a Number, we could accidentally add a Double to a List<Integer>.

With ? super T, adding T is safe because the collection is guaranteed to be T or one of its supertypes.

Therefore, I would use ? extends T for a producer/read-only use case and ? super T for a consumer/write use case.

